{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2131201979/1/codegen_prompts/4.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/1`\n- 题号：`4`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2131201979/1/4.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"4\">\n<description>\n将网格中由不同颜色连通区域组成的\"拼图碎片\"按照边界颜色匹配规则拼接，然后根据拼接后形成的封闭回路内外关系重新着色。\n\nInput structure：\n- 背景为黑色。\n- 网格中散布着若干矩形色块，每个色块由两种颜色组成：内部填充色（占据大部分面积）和边缘标记色（沿色块的一条或两条边绘制，宽度为 1 像素）。\n- 每种边缘标记色恰好出现在两个不同色块上（即标记色成对出现）。\n- 色块之间互不接触，间隔至少 2 像素背景。\n\nRule：\n- 找到所有边缘标记色的配对：对每种标记色，找到共享该标记色的两个色块。\n- 将配对的两个色块平移，使它们的同色标记边紧密相邻（标记边面对面贴合，标记像素相邻但不重叠）。拼接顺序：从网格中最左上的色块开始，依次拼接。\n- 拼接完成后，所有标记色像素重新着色为灰色，形成灰色边界线。\n- 检查灰色边界线是否围成了封闭区域：\n  - 被灰色边界线完全包围的内部填充区域保持原色不变。\n  - 未被灰色边界线完全包围的填充区域（即与网格边界连通）重新着色为 Azure。\n- 输出为拼接后所有色块的最小外接矩形（紧凑裁剪），背景为黑色。\n\nKey insight：边缘标记色是拼接配对的密钥（第一层推理），而拼接后灰色边界的拓扑封闭性决定了区域的最终着色（第二层推理）——解题者必须先完成空间拼接，再判断封闭关系。\n</description>\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/a6f40cea, train/278e5215, eval/9385bd28, train/57edb29d, eval/62593bfd, train/83eb0a57, eval/88e364bc, train/6cbe9eb8\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a6f40cea\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3656\nmechanism=0.4457\nlexical=0.0127\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:counter, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:crop_resize, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:adjacent, relation:between, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/a6f40cea.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a6f40cea.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nComplete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.\n- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.\n\nRule:\n- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.\n- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.\n- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.\n- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.\n\nKey insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"touch point s on closed rectangular frame where an open rectangular frame touches it\",\n  \"dst\": \"connect to another touch point d on closed rectangular frame where an open rectangular frame touches it\",\n  \"path\": \"path to complete the open rectangular frame\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"same color and pattern as the open rectangular frame\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to only retain inner area of the closed rectangular frame\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"278e5215\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3627\nmechanism=0.4035\nlexical=0.0129\ncode_feature=0.7143\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:output_resize, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:crop_resize, operation:recolor, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/278e5215.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/278e5215.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor the large mask crop column-by-column using the separate three-row palette strip.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- One large monochrome foreground object is the mask; its bbox is the output region and may contain background holes.\n- The remaining foreground is a 3-row palette rectangle with the same width as the mask bbox: two identical color rows and one uniform row.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the mask object's bbox.\n- For each output column, color mask pixels with the corresponding color from the repeated palette rows.\n- Replace background holes inside the crop with the palette's uniform third-row color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe grey object is only a stencil; its columns select colors from the legend, while its holes expose the legend's fill color.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"palette column s in the separate 3-row strip\",\n  \"dst\": \"column d in the mask bbox crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.column == d.column_in_crop\",\n  \"transform\": \"recolor mask pixels in d with s.repeated_row_color; fill background holes with the palette's uniform third-row color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"3-row palette strip maps each mask-crop column to its mask color; the uniform third row gives the hole color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to mask bbox\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9385bd28\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3538\nmechanism=0.4508\nlexical=0.0114\ncode_feature=0.3750\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, code:output_resize, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:flood_fill, operation:recolor, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/9385bd28.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9385bd28.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill rectangles between diagonal marker pairs using a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with a vertical legend at the bottom-left: each row is a (marker_color, fill_color) pair.\n- Scattered L-shaped corner markers of each marker color define opposite corners of axis-aligned rectangles.\n- Some markers are solid 2×2 blocks instead of corner pairs.\n\nRule:\n- Read legend bottom-to-top; for each entry find all pixels of that marker color.\n- Determine which two bbox corners carry L-shaped anchors — the *other* diagonal defines the fill region.\n- Fill the diamond (all shortest-path cells between those two corners) with the legend's fill color.\n- Solid 2×2 marker blocks are simply recolored to the fill color.\n- Later legend entries are protected from earlier fills.\n\nKey insight: The L-shaped anchors sit on the marker diagonal; the fill goes on the opposite diagonal of the bounding box.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color legend at bottom-left to find diagonal marker pairs of matching color, then fill the rectangle between them with the adjacent legend color.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"diagonal pixel pair s with same color forming opposite corners of a rectangle (upper corner pixel)\",\n  \"dst\": \"diagonal pixel pair d with same color forming opposite corners of a rectangle (lower corner pixel)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.color && s and d are diagonally opposite\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill rectangle bounded by s and d with color from legend where legend.left == s.color gives fill_color = legend.right\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"legend is vertical stack of color pairs at bottom-left, each pair (left-right only) has marker color on left and fill color on right\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: identify legend as vertically stacked color pairs at bottom-left corner; Step 2: process legend entries from bottom to top; Step 3: for each legend entry (marker_color on left, fill_color on right), find all diagonal pairs of marker_color pixels; Step 4: fill rectangle between each diagonal pair with fill_color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-legend\",\n    \"diagonal-markers\",\n    \"rectangle-fill\",\n    \"ordered-processing\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"sometimes as 2x2 squares which count as two diagonal triangles; processing order matters for overlapping regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"57edb29d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3603\nmechanism=0.4254\nlexical=0.0125\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:output_resize, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:shape_match, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/57edb29d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/57edb29d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The boundary color is the background/separator.\n- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.\n- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.\n- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.\n- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"edge-relative marker run s in a marked rectangle aligned with the plain rectangle row band\",\n  \"dst\": \"run d in the plain rectangle crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.nearest_edge_offsets == d.nearest_edge_offsets\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each matched d with the shared marker color; keep base rectangle fill elsewhere\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as the plain rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3253\nmechanism=0.3486\nlexical=0.0126\ncode_feature=0.7143\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:output_resize, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:shape_match, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83eb0a57\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3469\nmechanism=0.4403\nlexical=0.0126\ncode_feature=0.3750\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:crop_resize, operation:shape_match, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, output:crop, relation:inside, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/83eb0a57.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/83eb0a57.json\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"规则歧义集中在“封闭区域”这一层。我在做一个小规模搜索：找一类矩形拼块布局，使“每块只有 1-2 条标记边”的约束下，拼接后的灰线既能有闭环分支，也能有无闭环分支，而且 `main()` 能稳定反推。"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom collections import deque\nimport random\n\n\ndef neighbors(cell,H,W):\n    r,c=cell\n    for dr,dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):\n        nr,nc=r+dr,c+dc\n        if 0<=nr<H and 0<=nc<W:\n            yield nr,nc\n\ndef cell_adj(H,W):\n    cells=[(r,c) for r in range(H) for c in range(W)]\n    adj={c:set(neighbors(c,H,W)) for c in cells}\n    return cells,adj\n\ndef random_ham(H,W,cycle=False,tries=20000):\n    cells,adj=cell_adj(H,W)\n    n=len(cells)\n    for _ in range(tries):\n        start=random.choice(cells)\n        path=[start]\n        used={start}\n        ok=True\n        while len(path)<n:\n            cur=path[-1]\n            opts=[x for x in adj[cur] if x not in used]\n            # Warnsdorff-ish: fewest onward options first\n            opts.sort(key=lambda x: sum(1 for y in adj[x] if y not in used))\n            if not opts:\n                ok=False\n                break\n            # biased random among best few\n            k=min(3,len(opts))\n            nxt=random.choice(opts[:k])\n            path.append(nxt)\n            used.add(nxt)\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        if cycle and path[0] not in adj[path[-1]]:\n            continue\n        return path\n    return None\n\ndef enclosed_components(H,W,path,cycle=False):\n    wall=set()\n    seq=path+([path[0]] if cycle else [])\n    for a,b in zip(seq,seq[1:]):\n        wall.add(tuple(sorted((a,b))))\n    # unmarked adjacency graph between cells\n    cells=[(r,c) for r in range(H) for c in range(W)]\n    seen=set(); comps=[]\n    for cell in cells:\n        if cell in seen: continue\n        dq=[cell]; seen.add(cell); comp=[]; touch=False\n        while dq:\n            x=dq.pop(); comp.append(x)\n            r,c=x\n            if r==0 or c==0 or r==H-1 or c==W-1:\n                touch=True\n            for y in neighbors(x,H,W):\n                if tuple(sorted((x,y))) in wall:\n                    continue\n                if y not in seen:\n                    seen.add(y); dq.append(y)\n        comps.append((touch,comp))\n    return [comp for touch,comp in comps if not touch]\n\nfor H,W in [(3,3),(4,4),(4,5),(5,5)]:\n    found=[]\n    for cyc in [False,True]:\n        for _ in range(2000):\n            p=random_ham(H,W,cycle=cyc,tries=1)\n            if not p: continue\n            enc=enclosed_components(H,W,p,cycle=cyc)\n            if enc:\n                found.append((cyc,len(enc),[len(c) for c in enc],p))\n                break\n    print('grid',H,W,'found',[(cyc,ec,sz) for cyc,ec,sz,_ in found][:3])\n    if found:\n        cyc,ec,sz,p=found[0]\n        print(' sample cycle?',cyc,'sizes',sz,'path',p)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
